// error: `Shown` is a constructor trait, so it requires only constructor traits, and `Display` is a trait for types
// error: `Swapped` requires `Bimappable` at its own parameters, in order, as in `trait Swapped[A, B]: Bimappable[A, B]`
// error: `Varied` is a constructor trait, whose parameters are the holes of the constructors implementing it, so they take no variance; a constructor parameter states one on its hole, as in `F[+_]`
// error: `Point` takes no type arguments, so it is no constructor, and only a constructor implements the constructor trait `Mappable`
// error: `Sorted`'s parameter `T` is bounded by `Ord`, and `Mappable`'s parameter `A` is not, so `Sorted` is no constructor for it: what is `Mappable` applies its constructor to any type
// A constructor trait requires only constructor traits, at its own parameters in order, and its
// parameters take no variance. Only a generic type is a constructor, and one whose parameter is
// bounded is one only where the trait's parameter promises that bound.
trait Mappable[A]
  map[B](f: (A) -> B) -> Self[B]

trait Shown[A]: Mappable[A], Display

trait Bimappable[A, B]
  bimap[C, D](f: (A) -> C, g: (B) -> D) -> Self[C, D]

trait Swapped[A, B]: Bimappable[B, A]

trait Varied[+A]
  map[B](f: (A) -> B) -> Self[B]

data Point(x: int)

impl Mappable for Point

data Sorted[T: Ord](value: T)

impl Mappable for Sorted

main()
  println(1)
